@page "/components/fileupload"
@page "/components/fileuploader"

<DocsPage>
    <DocsPageHeader Title="File Upload" Component="@nameof(MudFileUpload<T>)">
        <Description>
            <MudText Typo="@Typo.body1" Class="my-4">
                Creating a <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> requires two key elements: 1) Component configuration and 2) Upload method implementation
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="@Typo.body1" Class="my-4">
                The <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> component provides a default implementation for an upload button and drag and drop area. It also provides a built in 
                <CodeInline>SelectedTemplate</CodeInline> to display files. For advanced usage you can use the <CodeInline>ActivatorContent</CodeInline> which provides a context
                for public <CodeInline>ClearAsync</CodeInline> and <CodeInline>OpenFilePickerAsync</CodeInline> methods that can be used as <CodeInline>OnClick</CodeInline> events.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="@Typo.body1" Class="my-4">
                After choosing the file(s), you will receive an <CodeInline>IReadOnlyList&#60IBrowserFile&#62</CodeInline> or <CodeInline>IBrowserFile</CodeInline> and you will need to manually handle the upload in <CodeInline>HandleFilesChanged</CodeInline>.
                Instead of using <CodeInline>multiple</CodeInline> to allow multiple files, you set T to <CodeInline>IReadOnlyList&#60IBrowserFile&#62</CodeInline>. Using <CodeInline>IBrowserFile</CodeInline> restricts the component to a single file.
            </MudText>
        </Description>
    </DocsPageHeader>
    <DocsPageContent>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Basic Usage (Single Button or Drag and Drop)">
                <Description>
                    Use a <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline>, set <CodeInline>T</CodeInline> and any options you want. For DragAndDrop functionality, set <CodeInline>DragAndDrop</CodeInline>
                    to true. By default it will use the full width and height of the parent container. If you need further customization you can target CSS selector <CodeInline>mud-file-upload-dragarea</CodeInline>
                    A <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> can be <CodeInline>Disabled</CodeInline> and it will disable its children automatically.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadBasicExample)">
                <FileUploadBasicExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Custom Content">
                <Description>
                    Use a <CodeInline>MudButton</CodeInline>, a <CodeInline>MudIconButton</CodeInline>, a <CodeInline>MudFab</CodeInline>, or any other content with the <CodeInline>CustomContent</CodeInline> render fragment.
                    <CodeInline>CustomContent</CodeInline> gets access to the <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> context to use the API. A <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> can be <CodeInline>Disabled</CodeInline> and 
                    it will disable its child button(s) automatically.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadButtonExample)">
                <FileUploadButtonExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="SelectedTemplate">
                <Description>
                    You can use the <CodeInline>SelectedTemplate</CodeInline> RenderFragment to customize the display of the current file(s). With access to the files through the API, the default <CodeInline>SelectedTemplate</CodeInline>
                    may not be necessary, to remove it use <CodeInline>&lt;SelectedTemplate /&gt;</CodeInline>.
                    Its <CodeInline>&#64;context</CodeInline> parameter matches <CodeInline>T</CodeInline>.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadSelectedTemplateExample)">
                <FileUploadSelectedTemplateExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Multiple and Accept">
                <Description>
                    To handle multiple files with <CodeInline>T="IReadOnlyList&#60IBrowserFile&#62"</CodeInline> or bind the <CodeInline>Files</CodeInline> property to an <CodeInline>IReadOnlyList&#60IBrowserFile&#62</CodeInline>. 
                    You can limit the valid file types with <CodeInline>Accept</CodeInline>.
                    To upload more than 10 files, you must specify a <CodeInline>MaximumFileCount</CodeInline>.

                    <MudAlert Class="my-3"
                              Dense
                              Severity="Severity.Info">
                        Note: Some browsers or platforms may require specific values for the <CodeInline>Accept</CodeInline> attribute.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        In MAUI using an Android device for example, you should use <CodeInline>Accept="image/png, image/jpg"</CodeInline> rather than <CodeInline>Accept=".png, .jpg"</CodeInline>.
                        <br>
                        Refer to the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/maui/platform-integration/storage/file-picker?view=net-maui-8.0&tabs=android#platform-differences">MAUI docs</a> for more information.
                    </MudAlert>
                </Description>

            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadMultipleAcceptExample)">
                <FileUploadMultipleAcceptExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Form Validation">
                <Description>
                    Use the <CodeInline>For</CodeInline> property to validate your files within a form, and bind your files to your model class using <CodeInline>&#64;bind-Files</CodeInline>.
                    You can then handle the file upload logic within your <CodeInline>MudForm</CodeInline> submit method.
                    The component provides a public <CodeInline>ClearAsync</CodeInline> method that you can use as <CodeInline>OnClick</CodeInline> handler for a button to clear files and update the form state.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadValidationExample)">
                <FileUploadValidationExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Event Options">
                <Description>
                    <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> offers two approaches to handle file changes:
                    - <CodeInline>Files</CodeInline>/<CodeInline>FilesChanged</CodeInline>: Recommended for MudForm integration
                    - <CodeInline>OnFilesChanged</CodeInline> callback: Preferred for custom implementation scenarios, it will fire regardless of validation by default, but can be configured to only fire when valid using <CodeInline>SuppressOnChangeWhenInvalid</CodeInline>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadEventOptionsExample)">
                <FileUploadEventOptionsExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Append Multiple Files">
                <Description>
                    With <CodeInline>AppendMultipleFiles</CodeInline> set to true, when additional files are selected they will be appended to the existing list.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadAppendMultipleExample)">
                <FileUploadAppendMultipleExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Drag and Drop Examples">
                <Description>
                    When using a <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline>, you can customize the look, feel, and usage as much as you want. <CodeInline>CustomContent</CodeInline> is provided as an area
                    for you to customize the buttons and/or drag and drop area to your liking. <CodeInline>CustomContent</CodeInline> contains a Context of the <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> for
                    access to ClearAsync or OpenPickerAsync and other API methods. Drag and Drop examples are very common, you can compose one using the DragAndDrop functionality of
                    <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> or completely roll your own.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionSubGroups>
                <DocsPageSection>
                    <SectionHeader Title="Using Built In Controls">
                        <Description>
                            The <CodeInline>MudFileUpload</CodeInline> component provides properties to manage drag-and-drop functionality and control drop zone visibility. You can customize your drop zone
                            inside <CodeInline>ChildContent</CodeInline> and set an <CodeInline>@@ondragenter</CodeInline> event to trigger the <CodeInline>Dragging</CodeInline> property. Once triggered
                            the drop zone becomes visible in the same space. When the drag ends, the drop zone disappears and <CodeInline>Dragging</CodeInline> is set to false by the control. 
                        </Description>
                    </SectionHeader>
                    <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadCustomExample)">
                        <FileUploadCustomExample />
                    </SectionContent>
                </DocsPageSection>
                <DocsPageSection>
                    <SectionHeader Title="Create a Custom Solution">
                        <Description>Alternatively, you can create your own custom solution by directly managing drag events and controlling the visibility of the input drop zone.</Description>
                    </SectionHeader>
                    <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadDragAndDropCustomScenarioExample)">
                        <FileUploadDragAndDropCustomScenarioExample />
                    </SectionContent>
                </DocsPageSection>
                <DocsPageSection>
                    <SectionHeader Title="With Form Validation">
                        <Description>
                            This example demonstrates how to combine drag-and-drop functionality with form validation, showing how to validate files before upload and provide user feedback.
                        </Description>
                    </SectionHeader>
                    <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadDragAndDropWithFormValidationExample)">
                        <FileUploadDragAndDropWithFormValidationExample />
                    </SectionContent>
                </DocsPageSection>
            <DocsPageSection>
                <SectionHeader Title="Different Examples">
                    <Description>The limit of customization is in the imagination.</Description>
                </SectionHeader>
                <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" ShowCode="false" Code="@nameof(FileUploadMiscExample)">
                    <FileUploadMiscExample />
                </SectionContent>
            </DocsPageSection>            
            </SectionSubGroups>
        </DocsPageSection>

    </DocsPageContent>
</DocsPage>
